WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.230
We're ASIAN KUNG-FU GENERATION, 
thank you for having us.

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:03.510
Thank you.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.760
"Please introduce yourselves."

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.580
"Tell us about how you decided on the band's 
name, as well as how you came to form a band."

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.460
I'm Kita, ASIAN KUNG-FU 
GENERATION's guitarist.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.000
Nice to meet you.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.300
I'm Gotoh, on guitar and vocal.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:29.930
I'm Yamada on bass.

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:32.550
I'm Ijichi on drums.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.900
How we decided on the name and 
how we came to be a band...

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:41.340
I came up with the name of the 
band before we formed the band.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:41.890
On your own.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:46.290
It was the name I wanted 
to use if I had a band.

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:54.050
I loved listening to 
Western music back then.

00:00:54.050 --> 00:01:04.000
And I had this big dream, 
almost a delusion of grandeur,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.770
which was to play music overseas, 
outside of Japan someday.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:13.880
So as a proof of our identity,

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:20.880
I felt it might be a good idea to 
include "Asian" in the band name.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.570
As for "Kung-fu", I was really into kung-fu 
films by stars like Jackie Chan and Bruce Lee,

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:30.320
so I wanted to have "kung-fu" in the name.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.320
As for "Generation", that just sounded cool,
so I just threw it in there at the end.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.420
A nice ring to it.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:40.940
Right, when we formed the band, the 
name had already been decided.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:41.460
Right.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:42.640
Two of us got together first.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:48.740
Kita-kun was waiting for me in the 
courtyard of the college at night.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:52.870
The day before, I was talking 
to him at the school music club

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:56.060
about maybe starting up our own band.

00:01:56.610 --> 00:02:01.190
And the next day, he came out of the 
darkness in the college courtyard and said,

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:03.960
"You said you wanted to 
start a band yesterday..."

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.270
That was his love call.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:07.840
And I said yes.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.760
I never thought it would last 
this long at that time.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:17.840
But he was a bit like a stalker, how 
he was waiting for me at night.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.830
I guess it was evening, the dim-lit evening.

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:25.320
I was taking evening 
classes at college, so...

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:27.020
That was scary. Then we invited Yama-chan.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:27.800
Yes.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:30.680
Kiyoshi was in the same club.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:31.550
Yes.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:34.690
And three years later, 
the four of us became a band.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:39.330
"It's been 25 years since 
the band was formed.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:43.420
What kind of efforts do you make 
to cater to both the fans who have

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:46.050
followed you since the
beginning and new fans?"

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:52.730
I think we try not to rank and 
differentiate fans that way.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:56.160
By that, I mean the fans 
who have been supporting us

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.510
for a long time and the fans 
who have just discovered us...

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:02.490
How should I put it? There is no difference.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:06.370
As long as they like the music 
we're playing right now...

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:10.200
Regardless of the timing, we welcome 
anyone who likes our music.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:16.700
Through our concerts, 
live sessions, or on screen,

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:21.380
if you like the feelings 
and sounds of our music,

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:24.500
we kind of see you as a part of our group.

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:29.430
So I think not judging the 
fans based on how long

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:34.640
they've been following us is 
the answer to this question.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.170
That's what I think.

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:41.570
"Your music has the influence 
from many genres of music,

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:48.860
such as 90's alternative 
rock, indie rock, and pop-punk.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:54.450
What is your favorite genre of music? Is 
there a genre that you'd like to try out?"

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:56.580
I bet we're all different.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.330
Let's start with Kiyoshi.

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:03.580
What is your favorite music genre, and 
is there a genre you'd like to try?

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:08.980
I've always loved metal.

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:10.520
Oh, you like metal.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:12.830
That's how I started playing in a band.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:19.870
And then I joined AKG, and only recently 
I started to like guitar rock.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:26.170
In the future, I kind of want to 
try out something like dance rock.

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:29.300
That's a sudden confession. Dance rock?

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.670
We have to talk it through.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:33.140
How about you, Yama-chan?

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:43.920
Well, when I listen to music that is being 
streamed, I tend to choose alternative rock.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:50.370
90's alternative rock is 
something I listened to a lot

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:55.720
since that's when I started 
to get into music.

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:58.390
So I go back to that often.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:00.560
And you?

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.440
Favorite genre for me?

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:07.800
Right now, I find rap music
interesting to listen to,

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:10.750
seeing what kind of beat 
is popular right now.

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:12.450
Or Latin music.

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:14.940
I listen to a variety of music.

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:16.330
Multinational music?

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:17.790
Yeah, I have a bunch of genres that I love.

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:19.710
I like Japanese traditional folk music too.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:21.310
What I want to try...

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:24.850
It's up to a discussion with the members,

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:28.530
so I can't proclaim here that I want 
to try dance rock, for example...

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:35.820
Right. For me, I'd say... my favorite 
playlist tends to be indie rock as well.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:37.360
You do like that stuff.

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:40.180
I think I tend to listen to a lot of U.S. indie rock.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:43.800
And what I'm interested in trying out is...

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:46.100
Like Kiyoshi mentioned dance rock,

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:51.790
a part of me thinks I want to try 
what my band members want to try.

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:56.630
I'm also on the lookout for 
what I want to do though...

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:58.490
That's where I'm at.

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:05.610
"From Summer Sonic, to Fuji Rock, Weezer 
Fes, and Nano Mugen Fes that you host,

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:08.590
you've been performing at 
a lot of live concerts.

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:15.620
What do live concerts mean for 
your overall music career?"

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:22.350
We create music and songs, 
and we release it.

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:25.400
Then we start talking about numbers, 
like how much it sold where,

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:31.030
where it is on the chart, or how much 
it's downloaded in which country.

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:35.570
But it makes us anxious because we 
can't really see the fans' faces.

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:38.110
Like, we don't know who these people are.

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:39.810
Even if we hear such numbers...

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:45.530
Exactly. But when we perform live, we see them 
right there, like when we performed in Brazil.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:51.590
I don't know if we'll ever 
get to visit Fortaleza again,

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:55.570
but we got to see people who were 
waiting for us to come and perform.

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:58.700
Seeing that in real life is really...

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:02.850
It gives us energy to create.

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:09.160
So, the fans may come to our 
concerts, wanting to see us.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:15.720
But for us, it's like where 
we get our energy from,

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:22.080
as well as the place where we get 
to witness our trusted fans.

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:24.240
So it's special.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:26.310
It makes me feel alive.

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:31.100
Honestly we were moved, to go and 
perform in Latin America.

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:33.970
Yes, we are moved wherever 
we go, like Asian countries.

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:39.440
We know that our fans are in Asia too 
through Facebook and such.

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:41.830
Yeah, like getting emails from them.

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:44.690
But seeing them from the stage was amazing.

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:45.930
Like, "they really do exist..."

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:47.260
It was moving.
Like, "they really do exist..."

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:48.740
They sang along too, which made me happy.
Like, "they really do exist..."

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:50.620
They sang along too, which made me happy.

00:07:51.830 --> 00:08:01.280
"What is your favorite tour? Do you have 
a plan to tour overseas in the future?"

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:03.030
Do you have a favorite tour?

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:04.250
Kiyoshi, how about you?

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:06.600
A favorite tour...

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:12.370
On the very first European tour of four 
countries or so, we went to London.

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:18.330
And that view of London left 
a strong impression on me.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:23.760
And there were so many fans in Paris.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:25.000
Yeah.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:26.320
That took us by surprise.

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:28.270
In 2015, if I remember correctly,

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:36.170
we toured Europe and then went to 
South America for like two weeks.

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:37.880
I remember that tour very well.

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:38.890
True.

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:44.410
And we got to do a North and South 
American tour two years later.

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:47.670
There are so many memories, 
and it was all great.

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:52.790
Performing overseas is one of 
the motivations for our band.

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:54.840
So we can tour internationally again.

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:59.150
It motivates us to create songs too.

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:07.780
"From &lt;i&gt;Naruto&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;BLEACH&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;Fullmetal 
Alchemist&lt;/i&gt; to &lt;i&gt;The Tatami Galaxy&lt;/i&gt;,

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:12.940
you have created many hit songs that are 
widely loved by many international anime fans.

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:17.550
You're pioneers of the 
anime OP/ED composers who had

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:20.720
a huge impact on international anime fans.

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:26.120
Did you expect that you would have this many 
international fans when you were creating these songs?"

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:28.060
Back when we made them?

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:35.400
When we made "Haruka Kanata," 
the theme song for &lt;i&gt;Naruto&lt;/i&gt;,

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:42.270
I remember speaking with a SONY director, 
and how we talked about the fact

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:49.710
that we got to do the OP for 
this show was so meaningful,

00:09:49.710 --> 00:09:53.410
and we could be on the world 
stage with this anime.

00:09:55.210 --> 00:10:03.330
It's an anime about ninja, and I thought 
it was very Japanese and interesting.

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:10.930
So I knew it would be popular overseas, 
but I never expected this much.

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:17.960
No matter where we go, &lt;i&gt;Naruto&lt;/i&gt; 
feels like it's on another level,

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:23.890
with how loved and watched the 
show is, all over the world.

00:10:23.890 --> 00:10:24.710
Even to this day.

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:28.480
Exactly. I'm reminded of how 
great of an anime that was.

00:10:28.990 --> 00:10:30.810
It's a fun show.

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:37.030
And recently, when we're asked to 
create songs for anime like that,

00:10:37.030 --> 00:10:40.070
we already have international 
fans in mind to some extent.

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:41.010
That's true.

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:43.260
It does affect how we create music.

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:47.580
When we made the song for &lt;i&gt;Naruto&lt;/i&gt;, it was 
almost like... how should I put it...

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:49.170
Ninja-like?

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:53.750
Right, Ninja-like. Something the
four of us have in common.

00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:58.880
We wanted to choose something a 
bit more oriental in scale.

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:00.320
Also in phrasing.
We wanted to choose something a 
bit more oriental in scale.

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:02.410
Like how YMO did so in the past.

00:11:02.410 --> 00:11:07.380
We thought that a slightly distorted 
Japanese melody might work better,

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:10.390
and it was reflected in 
the guitar riff and such.

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:12.570
And it's nice to hear the 
fans sing along in concerts.

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:14.520
Makes me happy.

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:15.700
So it does affect our music.

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:16.590
Absolutely.
So it does affect our music.

00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:17.360
Absolutely.

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:22.350
"Did you watch &lt;i&gt;Bocchi the Rock!&lt;/i&gt; ?

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:26.640
Every episode title is 
named after your songs,

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:31.030
and the characters' names 
also come from your names.

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:36.330
Are you involved in the 
manga or the anime series?"

00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:38.260
We didn't really...

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:39.670
We didn't actively approach them.

00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:43.580
But it made me really happy, 
as well as surprised.

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:47.490
It's already very popular overseas.

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:48.560
That's true.

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:56.750
There are scenes of Kanazawa Hakkei, 
where we spent our school life, or...

00:11:56.750 --> 00:11:57.400
The Yokohama South area.

00:11:57.400 --> 00:11:58.340
Yes, and Shimokitazawa in Tokyo.
The Yokohama South area.

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:02.940
Yes, and Shimokitazawa in Tokyo.

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:04.780
They also go to Enoshima.

00:12:04.780 --> 00:12:07.540
Right. They go to Kamakura as well.

00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:14.250
Those depictions of the areas that are very 
familiar to us were really fun to see.

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:18.330
The background animation was very realistic.

00:12:19.010 --> 00:12:26.430
The characters have our last names, but I 
don't think they have our personalities.

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:29.170
Yama-chan's character is 
kind of a cool character.

00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:31.220
Is that so?

00:12:31.750 --> 00:12:34.300
My character is super outgoing.

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:37.360
We're talking about the character, not you.

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:42.050
He looked like he got complimented.

00:12:42.580 --> 00:12:43.730
We're not complimenting you.

00:12:43.730 --> 00:12:47.260
Yamada-san in the show is 
a cool character, got it.

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:54.250
But maybe, the author of the 
series has those images of us?

00:12:54.250 --> 00:12:56.520
I don't think there's any correlation.

00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:05.350
"Are you aware that many metal core 
bands are influenced by "Haruka Kanata"?

00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:09.500
Many well-known international bands 
such as Bad Omens, Alpha Wolf,

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:17.240
and Archetypes Collide give "Haruka 
Kanata" as one of the reasons

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:21.030
why they started making heavy metal music."

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:22.800
That's so cool.

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:26.120
Bad Omens is a very famous band, isn't it?

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:27.360
Sorry, I don't know.

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:33.850
I believe they're really famous, 
and their music is on the heavy side.

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:40.110
I think they're kind of like 
My Chemical Romance.

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:45.990
But in the past, we once heard that 
"Haruka Kanata" was one of the...

00:13:46.780 --> 00:13:47.700
Thundercat said he used to listen to it?

00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:48.970
Right?
Thundercat said he used to listen to it?

00:13:49.590 --> 00:13:53.700
I know that "Haruka Kanata" was 
in Thundercat's playlist.

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:54.860
That was a surprise.

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:58.560
I've heard stories like that 
in the last several years,

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:02.980
and it was surprising that those who play 
hard music like that like our song.

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:06.710
I mean, it makes us happy to hear 
about fans, musicians or not.

00:14:06.710 --> 00:14:10.730
If they like our song and it inspired 
them to make something new.

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:12.000
Yeah, totally happy.

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:16.500
But we don't really think of our 
music as metal, so it's unexpected.

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:20.760
But that's the interesting 
thing about music.

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:27.360
Perhaps Yama-chan's heavy bass line in the 
beginning might have something in common.

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:29.630
In terms of the sound.

00:14:29.630 --> 00:14:30.740
That makes us happy.

00:14:31.460 --> 00:14:33.170
Our album, "Surf Bungaku Kamakura,"

00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:37.980
which we made in 2008, is making a 
come back as a complete version.

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:42.560
There's a train line in Japan 
called the Enoshima Electric Railway.

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:44.030
And it has 15 train stations.

00:14:44.030 --> 00:14:47.800
Out of those 15, we made ten songs in 2008.

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:51.470
And we're making five more songs 
for the rest of the stations,
We released an album "Surf Bungaku Kamakura Complete" in the summer of 2023 that is now available for streaming.

00:14:51.470 --> 00:14:54.710
and the album featuring a total of 
15 tracks is coming out this summer.
We released an album "Surf Bungaku Kamakura Complete" in the summer of 2023 that is now available for streaming.

00:14:54.710 --> 00:15:00.370
So we hope you listen to it on 
various streaming services.
We released an album "Surf Bungaku Kamakura Complete" in the summer of 2023 that is now available for streaming.

00:15:00.370 --> 00:15:03.870
It's a power pop album 
reminiscent of Weezer.

00:15:03.870 --> 00:15:12.650
That's true. So if you're a fan of 90's 
American alternative rock, you'll like this one.

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:16.300
Lots of homages throughout the 
album, so please give it a listen.

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:30.360
From here, we're supposed to answer 
questions individually in one sentence.

00:15:30.730 --> 00:15:33.020
Let's start with Kiyoshi 
and go down the line.

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:34.460
Are you ready?

00:15:37.540 --> 00:15:41.160
"What is the first song on 
your favorite playlist?"

00:15:42.550 --> 00:15:47.210
Metallica's "72 Seasons."

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:56.650
The Lemon Twigs' "Any Time of Day."

00:15:56.650 --> 00:15:59.230
I listen to that a lot, although it's 
not the first song on my playlist.

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:00.780
You, Gotch-san?

00:16:01.670 --> 00:16:06.620
Anohni's "It Must Change."

00:16:06.620 --> 00:16:08.360
Everyone has one.

00:16:08.360 --> 00:16:15.790
For me, Foo Fighters' "Under 
You" is in my favorite playlist.

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:19.760
"Is there an artist or song 
that you're secretly into?"

00:16:20.150 --> 00:16:22.610
I like Cory Wong these days.

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:26.040
He comes to Fuji Rock.

00:16:27.050 --> 00:16:28.540
